1.7 Hands-on exercise
Review the unit-number Jira story and list every behavior GitHub Copilot would otherwise have to guess. Could you classify each item as one of the following?

a business requirement;
an implementation decision;
a test or verification question; or
an explicit non-goal.

8.4 Backend proposal
The backend proposal could connect the business request to the repository change. It would state that the capability adds unit-number search, name the affected service boundary, and list related search capabilities that remain out of scope. The rationale can stay short because the requirements and scenarios will carry the behavior.
The backend delta specification could define the observable contract selected during exploration. For each requirement, it would include scenarios for the agreed valid input, invalid input, no match, and any other case that the decision record marks as necessary. Each scenario should describe the request and observable response without prescribing a controller class or database query.
The design can then record the implementation boundary, perhaps including:

REST endpoint and request mapping;
validation location and error model;
service and persistence boundaries;
OpenAPI publication;
tests that demonstrate the specified scenarios.

The design need not become a second requirements document. If a design choice changes observable behavior, it should return to the specification for review rather than remain hidden in implementation prose.

8.5 Frontend proposal
The frontend proposal could name the user-facing search capability and its scope. It would refer to the backend-owned contract rather than describe a second version of the API. The frontend delta specification should describe what the user can enter, what appears while the request is running, and what the user sees for a result, an empty response, or an error.
The frontend design should record the Angular boundaries that implement those scenarios:

form and input validation;
generated-client integration;
loading, result, empty, and error state handling;
component or service boundaries;
tests for the specified interaction.

Client generation would follow the availability of the backend contract. The frontend proposal can describe that dependency, but it should avoid inventing request types or duplicating the OpenAPI contract by hand.

8.9 Verification checkpoint
The proposal is ready to apply when the following checks hold:

every implementation-blocking question from exploration has an answer, owner, or explicit deferral;
each accepted requirement has observable scenarios;
each scenario maps to a design decision and at least one task;
the backend owns the OpenAPI contract;
contract publication precedes client generation;
the frontend describes states that match the backend responses; and
no task introduces behavior outside the reviewed scope.

If a check fails, the artifact that owns the missing information is usually the right place to revise. A missing requirement is better repaired there than through a more detailed implementation task.

9.3 Backend implementation
The backend design can be applied across the boundaries it names:

validation at the agreed API boundary;
controller behavior and response mapping;
service behavior for the approved unit-number capability;
repository access through the existing persistence boundary; and
error behavior described by the specification and design.

Where the repository design calls for them, use Spring Data JPA and H2. Keep those persistence choices in the implementation. The behavioral requirements can describe what a client sends and observes without specifying which repository interface or database query supplies the result.
Keep the change within the approved unit-number scope. If implementation reveals a related search behavior that the proposal did not cover, record it as a question or follow-up. That keeps the new decision separate from the change currently under review.

10.8 Verification checkpoint
The Angular change can move to cross-repository verification once the following evidence is available:

the generated client comes from the reviewed backend document;
generated files are isolated from hand-written application code;
the UI uses generated request and response types rather than duplicate models;
invalid input, loading, result, no-match, and error behavior each have evidence;
component tests assert visible behavior and meaningful request boundaries; and
the Playwright path exercises the configured frontend and backend together.

If the generated client and the specification disagree, leave the task open while the backend contract is inspected. If the contract matches but a UI scenario is missing, the frontend implementation or test needs attention. The next chapter can then validate the two repositories together and decide whether the changes are ready to synchronize and archive.

11.6 Archive only completed work
Archiving closes the active change after its accepted behavior has been synchronized and verified. Ideally, it leaves a future reader enough context to understand what changed, why it changed, and where the supporting evidence came from.
Before archiving the backend and frontend changes, confirm that:

artifact validation succeeds in both repositories;
implementation tasks are complete;
focused tests and the cross-repository path pass;
main specifications are coherent after synchronization; and
the change record preserves its Jira and cross-repository identifiers.

Archive the changes using the repository’s configured OpenSpec workflow. Inspect the archive after the operation. A moved directory would not be sufficient if the archive had lost the proposal, requirements, design, tasks, or the reason for the change.
You will generally want to archive the two repositories only when their completion states agree. If the backend is ready but the frontend still has an open scenario, consider keeping the active work open and recording the missing evidence. The archive is most useful when it describes completed work rather than a hopeful stopping point.

13.6 Verification checkpoint
Search the specifications, code, generated artifacts, UI text, and tests for obsolete 15-character assumptions. Then inspect the final source-of-truth result that archive will produce.
The change could be ready to close when:

the accepted requirement has one complete replacement rather than two contradictory rules;
the maximum-of-10 behavior and its boundary scenarios pass;
normalization and empty-input behavior match the reviewed decisions;
backend and frontend validation describe the same constraint;
generated artifacts no longer carry the old constraint; and
preserved search, match, no-match, and error behavior still has verification evidence.

If the search finds an obsolete assumption, the change can stay open while the affected artifact, code, generated output, or test is updated and the focused verification is repeated. Archiving would make sense only after the accepted specification and the implementation describe the same behavior.

14.7 Verification checkpoint
Search the active artifacts, specifications, tasks, code, generated output, tests, and UI text for assumptions from the original one-filter design. Then verify the revised scenarios, including the cases where one or both filters have no selection and where both filters are applied together.
You can consider closing the change when:

the active artifact states one coherent filter behavior;
the combination and empty-selection rules are explicit;
the API, query, generated client, and UI use the same behavior;
stale tasks and prose no longer describe only the original filter; and
tests provide evidence for the revised scenarios and the behavior that remains unchanged.

If the search finds an obsolete assumption, leave the change open. Update the artifact or implementation that owns the assumption, then repeat the focused verification. The change can be archived after the accepted specification and the implementation describe the same behavior.

15.4 Compare deltas before archive
One useful way to review each active change is twice: first against the accepted baseline, then against the other active changes. The first comparison asks what the change adds, modifies, or preserves. The second asks whether another proposal adds, modifies, or removes the same behavior.
Start with the behavioral requirements, not the file list. For each requirement, record:

the accepted behavior it assumes;
the behavior it changes or adds;
the shared contract or UI surface it touches; and
the evidence that will show the behavior works.

Then compare the records. Look for more than identical words. These are overlap signals:

both changes alter the same endpoint or generated client method;
both changes define a different validation rule for the same input;
both changes change how the UI selects a search mode;
one change assumes a result shape that the other changes; or
a regression test in one change would fail after the other change is synchronized.

When a comparison finds overlap, return to the requirements and scenarios. Resolving the disagreement through implementation order alone could make the behavior accidental. The team could combine the changes, revise one proposal, or keep both with an explicit compatibility decision. The choice belongs in the active artifacts so a reviewer can recover it without the originating chat session.

15.8 Verification checkpoint
One useful checkpoint is to validate the active artifacts before implementation, then validate the final main specifications after synchronization. Run the regression tests for existing search modes as well as the tests for the two reconciled changes. Include contract generation and frontend tests when the shared API or client changed.
The work is ready to close when the evidence supports all of the following:

each active change has a distinct business purpose and an explicit scope;
shared requirements have one agreed interpretation;
the accepted specifications contain no contradictory search rules;
the generated client reflects the reviewed backend contract;
the UI and validation match the selected search behavior; and
affected new and regression scenarios have evidence from tests.

If the second change still refers to the pre-archive baseline, keep it active. Update the stale artifact, implementation, or test, then repeat the focused verification. Isolation makes the work possible; comparison and synchronization are what can make the result coherent.

Windows 11 or macOS;
Git and access to the two prepared Bitbucket repositories;
VS Code or IntelliJ IDEA with GitHub Copilot Chat access;
OpenSpec 1.13.1;
Node.js 24.19.0 and npm;
Java 21 and Maven for the Spring Boot 4.1.1 project;
Angular 21, Vitest, Playwright, and OpenAPI Generator for the frontend project;
Quarto only if participants will render the book locally.
